"Look closely at the back of your hand. It seems smooth and solid. But if you could look much, much closer, you would find that your skin is built of tiny separate pieces, packed together like bricks in a wall. Each piece is alive. Each one is called a cell.": "9d7ffe739c3b",
"Look closely at the back of your hand. It seems smooth and solid. But if you could look much, much closer, you would find that your skin is built of tiny separate pieces, packed together like bricks in a wall. Each piece is alive. Each one is called a cell. Cells were first seen in 1665. An English scientist named Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork through a microscope and saw rows of little empty boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms that monks lived in, which were called cells, so that is the name he gave them. About 170 years later, scientists came to understand that every plant and every animal is made of cells. A cell is like a very small bag. Its skin is called the cell membrane, and it decides what may come in and what may go out. The bag is filled with a watery jelly. Floating in the jelly is the nucleus, which keeps the cell's instructions. There are also many tiny power stations, called mitochondria, which turn food into energy that the cell can use. Plant cells have all of these, and three things more. Around the membrane there is a stiff wall, which gives the plant its strength. Inside there are green chloroplasts, which make food from sunlight. And in the middle there is a large bag of water, called a vacuole, which keeps the cell firm. Some living things are just one cell, alone. A drop of pond water is full of them. Larger living things are made of a great many cells. An adult human body has about thirty trillion. They are not all alike. Nerve cells, muscle cells, skin cells, and blood cells each have a different shape because each has a different job. Where do new cells come from? Always from other cells. A cell grows, copies its instructions, and divides into two. That is how you grew from a single cell into the person you are, and it is how your skin mends itself after a cut. Every cell alive today came from a cell that lived before it.": "f10ff3f9d2fb",
"One cell divides into two.": "3f180eea7ed0",
